From: Andrei <andrei@luta.email>
Subject: [PATCH] MinGW: target SSE2 like the Visual Studio 2022 x86 build does

Visual Studio 2022 compiles 32-bit x86 with /arch:SSE2 by default, so the
community Windows builds already require SSE2 (every x86 CPU since 2004).
GCC's i686 default is plain x87 without SSE2, which makes the MinGW build
both slower and unable to use the SSE2 code paths in the engine. Only the
instruction set is enabled; scalar floating point stays on x87 so the
MinGW build keeps the same precision behaviour as before.
---
 cmake/mingw.cmake | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/cmake/mingw.cmake b/cmake/mingw.cmake
--- a/cmake/mingw.cmake
+++ b/cmake/mingw.cmake
@@ -22,6 +22,9 @@ if(MINGW)
     # Compatibility flags for legacy code
     add_compile_options(
         -fno-strict-aliasing        # Avoid type-punning issues with DX8/COM
+        -msse2                      # Match the VS2022 x86 default (/arch:SSE2); required for the
+                                    # SSE2 intrinsics used in Dependencies/Utility and enables
+                                    # vectorized code paths. Scalar FP math stays on x87.
     )
 
     # MSVC compatibility macros for MinGW
